# JARVIS — example environment file
# ===================================================================
# This file documents local development settings and public cloud client settings.
# Product direction is cloud-first: the iPhone connects directly to Supabase; Windows
# and the local bridge are optional development/local-tool environments. The frontend defaults to the authenticated cloud backend; the local bridge is opt-in.
# Copy this to .env.local, and add only what you actually want to change.
#
# All model inference must go through the approved FreeLLMAPI gateway or the
# authenticated cloud provider route. Paid Claude/Anthropic, Gemini API Free Tier,
# ElevenLabs and subscription-backed account connectors are disabled.
#
# Two audiences read these variables:
#   - the BRIDGE (the Node server, bridge/server.mjs) reads the JARVIS_* and
#     FREELLMAPI_* variables from its shell environment.
#   - the FRONTEND (the Vite app) reads the VITE_* variables from .env.local.
# Only VITE_-prefixed values ever reach the browser.


# ===================================================================
# BRIDGE (the server) — read from the shell that runs `npm run bridge`
# ===================================================================
# These are shown with their defaults. Leave them unset to accept the default.

# Port the bridge listens on (WebSocket + a few HTTP endpoints). Default 8787.
# If you change it, change VITE_BRIDGE_URL to match.
# JARVIS_BRIDGE_PORT=8787

# Interface the bridge listens on. Default 127.0.0.1 — this machine only.
# Setting anything else puts the agent on your network; don't, unless you have
# put your own authentication in front of it.
# JARVIS_BRIDGE_HOST=127.0.0.1

# Extra hostnames the bridge answers to, comma separated. Requests addressed to
# any other name are refused, which is what stops DNS rebinding. Default: none
# beyond localhost, 127.0.0.1 and [::1].
# JARVIS_ALLOWED_HOSTS=

# Select the local inference backend. FreeLLMAPI is mandatory; no Claude/Anthropic
# subscription or paid API fallback is available. Startup fails if the key is absent.
# Its URL is deliberately restricted to loopback so the unified key cannot be
# sent to a remote host by mistake.
# JARVIS_PROVIDER=freellmapi
# FREELLMAPI_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:3001
# FREELLMAPI_API_KEY=freellmapi-your-unified-key
# FREELLMAPI_MODEL=auto
#
# When JARVIS_PROVIDER=freellmapi, make sure the FreeLLMAPI router itself has
# only free-tier provider credentials enabled. FreeLLMAPI can also route custom
# endpoints; JARVIS cannot verify their billing terms. No paid fallback is
# configured by this integration.
#
# Allow effectful tools (send, tap, delete, install, pay). Off by default, so
# JARVIS can look at anything and change nothing. `npm run bridge:writes` turns
# it on for you (via --writes). Read the decideTool gate in bridge/policy.mjs first.
# JARVIS_ALLOW_WRITES=0

# Which MCP servers from ~/.claude.json JARVIS gets, comma separated. This is
# a mandatory allowlist: unset or empty passes none. Name only servers whose
# permissions and billing terms have been reviewed. Default: none.
# JARVIS_MCP_SERVERS=

# Built-in capabilities to leave out, comma separated: browser (your own
# signed-in Chrome), camera, interface (the ui_* retinting tools). Left-out
# tools never reach the model, which also trims every turn's input. Default:
# none.
# JARVIS_DISABLE=

# Extra browser origins allowed to open the agent WebSocket, comma separated.
# Local dev origins are already allowed; add others only if you serve the app
# from a different address. Default: none.
# JARVIS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=

# Allow clients that send no Origin header at all (curl, scripts, native apps).
# Off by default, because that is also what local malware looks like. Default 0.
# JARVIS_ALLOW_NO_ORIGIN=0

# The agent's working directory. Its file tools (Read, Grep, Glob, and Write /
# Edit when writes are on) are confined to this folder, the system temp
# directory and JARVIS_FILE_ROOTS. Credential locations (~/.ssh, ~/.claude,
# .env files …) are refused even inside those. Default: ~/.jarvis/workspace.
# JARVIS_WORKSPACE=

# Extra directories the file tools may use and the /file endpoint may serve
# images from, comma separated — a Pictures folder, an external drive of
# footage. Default: none.
# JARVIS_FILE_ROOTS=

# Keep a transcript of every session, the way Claude Code does, under
# ~/.claude/projects. Off by default: nothing reads them back, and they hold
# every tool result verbatim. Default 0.
# JARVIS_KEEP_TRANSCRIPTS=0

# Run the local Agent SDK runtime through this executable instead of its
# bundled CLI — typically a sandbox wrapper. Model inference still routes only
# through FreeLLMAPI; this setting does not enable Claude account billing.
# JARVIS_CLAUDE_EXECUTABLE=

# Log every message the agent sends, including answer text, tool calls and the
# MCP servers that connected, to the bridge's terminal. For debugging only.
# Default 0.
# JARVIS_DEBUG=0

# WSL only: the name of the Windows pipe the browser relay connects to.
# Default: claude-mcp-browser-bridge- followed by your Windows user name.
# JARVIS_CHROME_PIPE=

# Paid ElevenLabs speech endpoints are disabled. Local speech output uses the
# operating system; cloud STT is subject to per-account consent and server quota.


# ===================================================================
# FRONTEND (Vite) — put these in .env.local
# ===================================================================

# Backend selector. Only cloud or bridge are supported; direct browser-to-provider
# calls are disabled to prevent paid API keys entering the browser bundle.
#   cloud  — authenticated Supabase cloud brain. Default.
#   bridge — local development mode; requires FreeLLMAPI and `npm run bridge`.
# VITE_BACKEND=cloud
# For local bridge development, set VITE_BACKEND=bridge.

# Where the bridge listens. Keep the port in step with JARVIS_BRIDGE_PORT above.
# Default ws://localhost:8787.
# VITE_BRIDGE_URL=ws://localhost:8787

# Browser text-to-speech engine, used when no ElevenLabs voice is active.
# Default system.
#   system — the browser's built-in speechSynthesis. Instant, on-device.
#   kokoro — a neural voice running in the browser. Better sound, slower to start.
# VITE_TTS_ENGINE=system

# Which Kokoro voice, when VITE_TTS_ENGINE=kokoro. Default bm_george.
# VITE_KOKORO_VOICE=bm_george

# Paid ElevenLabs speech is disabled; this setting is ignored.
# Browser speech synthesis is the zero-cost local TTS fallback.
# VITE_USE_ELEVENLABS=false

# Supabase Cloud runtime. The Cloud Access panel activates the cloud brain after sign-in.
# Cloud inference and speech providers are configured as Supabase Edge Function
# secrets and are consent/quota-gated server-side. Do not use Gemini API Free Tier
# for the EEA-hosted JARVIS app. Never put provider keys in VITE_*.
# The publishable key is designed for browser use; NEVER put service_role/secret keys in VITE_*.
# Cloud web sessions are memory-only and end when the page reloads.
VITE_SUPABASE_URL=https://undmhmdwhxvjgvezvucy.supabase.co
# Optional isolated Edge Function for a dedicated preview deployment. Production defaults to jarvis-api.
# VITE_SUPABASE_FUNCTION_NAME=jarvis-preview-api
VITE_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=sb_publishable_oJvnF8wB4y1mrFWzlzSZOQ_kFy-jzVx

# No direct Anthropic or ElevenLabs API keys are accepted by the frontend.
